proposal.text:
{
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "At the initial open-hand approach for a requested green cube, the demonstration had moved its covering container to the left side of the front image. Memory correctly identified that container by its final silhouette centre and preserved the settled demonstration frame. The policy selected the right container, approach height, and open gripper, but its estimated horizontal centre missed the recorded approach by slightly more than 1 cm. This is a SUFFICIENCY failure in the geometry-to-waypoint instruction, not an identity or memory-transition failure.\n\nStrengthen the existing top-face reconstruction rule with a concrete two-view fitting procedure. When both calibrated current cameras show the stationary selected container, match its physical top-face corners between the front and wrist images before estimating the approach centre. Distinguish top-face boundaries from lower silhouette edges and shaded side faces. Parameterize the top as a horizontal square with unknown centre (x,y,h), side length s, and world yaw theta: its four corners are (x,y,h) plus the yaw-rotated offsets (±s/2,±s/2,0). Project those same four world corners through each camera's supplied calibration and jointly fit the parameters to the observed corresponding corners and physical edges. Resolve corner-order ambiguity by testing cyclic correspondences and retaining the fit consistent with both views and the visible side faces. Use all usable corners rather than estimating the centre or yaw from the small front-image face alone. If a corner is occluded or clipped, constrain it by the fitted square and visible edges; do not substitute an image boundary. Check the fitted projections against both images before accepting the centre. Use the fitted x,y for the open approach at z=0.20 m and derive yaw from the fitted world edges, choosing the square-equivalent orientation nearest the current hand orientation. Refine the same geometry before the closing descent. Retain the existing stationary-reference fallback when current observations are unusable, and retain all existing identity, lift-count, grasp, return, and withdrawal rules. No episode-specific coordinates or additional memory fields are needed."
}

